WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Thread-safety documentation has to be precise.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:15.080
Saying a library is thread-safe is too vague unless the contract explains which operations may run concurrently, which objects are protected, which state is shared, and which responsibilities belong to the caller.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.660
Different objects may have different rules, and read-only operations may not be safe if they touch lazy global state.

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.000
Consumers need language they can translate into their own locking rules.

5
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.480
Hidden shared mutable state is a common source of surprise.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:39.100
Caches, registries, global configuration, static initialization, background workers, and singleton-like helpers should be designed and documented carefully.

7
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:46.400
If shared state is required, the locking strategy and shutdown behavior should be part of the library design rather than an implementation accident.

8
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.760
Callbacks need special care.

9
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:57.100
Calling user code while holding internal locks can create deadlocks, reentrancy surprises, and unpredictable state transitions.

10
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:07.920
The library should document whether callbacks can be invoked concurrently, whether they may call back into the library, which thread owns the call, and what lifetime guarantees apply to callback data.

11
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.340
If callbacks are deferred, queueing and cancellation rules should be clear.

12
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.020
Concurrency contracts should be tested.

13
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:27.200
Unit tests, stress tests, sanitizer-supported builds, and targeted integration scenarios can exercise concurrent reads, writes, cancellation, shutdown, and repeated initialization.

14
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.180
Secure libraries make concurrency guarantees explicit so consumers do not have to infer them from private code paths.

15
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.960
Release notes should call out changes to locking, callbacks, and background workers.

16
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.220
If guarantees change, consumers need enough warning to update their own synchronization assumptions.

17
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:55.040
The library should also document whether objects are safe to move, share, or destroy while work is active or callbacks are pending under load and during shutdown.
